<% content_for :title, "About — SceneCore" %>

<section class="px-4 sm:px-6 py-12 sm:py-16 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
  <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-neutral-500 mb-3">About</p>
  <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-4">
    Your band. Your fans. Your home.
  </h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-neutral-300 mb-12 max-w-2xl">
    SceneCore is a music-first home for independent bands — the place where a
    band's music, its people and everything it shares with them stay together.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-2">The problem</h2>
  <p class="text-neutral-400 mb-8 max-w-2xl">
    A band is discovered on one service, publishes on another, sells somewhere
    else, and talks to its fans through two or three more.
  </p>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-16">
    <%= render layout: "info_card" do %>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">It isn't only scattered tools</h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-300">
        Every one of those services is good at its part. What breaks is the
        relationship itself: it ends up split across places the band does not
        own, on terms the band does not set.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%= render layout: "info_card" do %>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">A rented audience is a fragile one</h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-300">
        When the people who care about a band live inside someone else's
        platform, reaching them depends on that platform continuing to allow
        it. A following built this way can thin out without anyone leaving.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-2">What SceneCore does</h2>
  <p class="text-neutral-400 mb-8 max-w-2xl">
    It gives that relationship one continuous home, with the music at its
    centre.
  </p>

  <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 mb-16">
    <%= render layout: "info_card" do %>
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">For bands</h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-300 text-sm">
        A page that is yours: publish your albums, write to the people
        following you, share what only members see, sell merchandise, announce
        shows, and see how that relationship is going — without deciding first
        which service each piece belongs to.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%= render layout: "info_card" do %>
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">For fans</h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-300 text-sm">
        Discover a band, listen, follow, keep up with what it publishes, and
        support it directly if you want to — without leaving the band's own
        page to do each of those things.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%= render layout: "info_card" do %>
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Music first, not content in general</h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-300 text-sm">
        A band is not a generic creator with a posting schedule. The music is
        what the relationship is built on, so it stays at the centre of the
        page rather than becoming one more kind of post.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-2">What SceneCore is not</h2>
  <p class="text-neutral-400 mb-8 max-w-2xl">
    Being clear about this is more useful than claiming to replace everything.
  </p>

  <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 mb-16">
    <%= render layout: "info_card" do %>
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Not a streaming replacement</h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-300 text-sm">
        Streaming services are where most people find new music, and that is
        worth keeping. SceneCore links out to them rather than asking anyone to
        listen somewhere new. Use them to be discovered; use SceneCore to build
        on it.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%= render layout: "info_card" do %>
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Not a store with extra features</h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-300 text-sm">
        Selling a record is one moment in a relationship, not the whole of it.
        Commerce here sits inside the band's page because the people buying are
        the same people following, reading and showing up.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%= render layout: "info_card" do %>
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Not a social network</h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-300 text-sm">
        There is no feed ranking bands against each other and no algorithm
        deciding who hears from whom. A fan follows a band and hears from that
        band.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-2">How we make money</h2>
  <p class="text-neutral-400 mb-8 max-w-2xl">
    Bands pay nothing to be here. SceneCore takes a share only when a band
    earns.
  </p>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-4">
    <%= render layout: "info_card" do %>
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Bands keep 85% of memberships</h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-300 text-sm">
        The remaining 15% covers running the platform: hosting the music and
        content, handling payments, and keeping the band's home online.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%= render layout: "info_card" do %>
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Bands keep 90% of store sales</h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-300 text-sm">
        The band supplies and ships the goods, so the platform takes less.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <p class="text-sm text-neutral-500 mb-16">
    <%= link_to "How it works", how_it_works_path, class: "text-yellow-300 hover:text-yellow-200 transition" %>
    goes through the membership levels and the money side in full.
  </p>

  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
    <%= link_to "Discover bands", discover_bands_path, class: "rounded-full bg-yellow-400/10 text-yellow-300 border border-yellow-500/30 font-semibold px-6 py-3 hover:bg-yellow-400/20 hover:text-yellow-200 transition" %>
    <% unless user_signed_in? %>
      <%= link_to "Create your band's home", new_user_registration_path, class: "rounded-full border border-neutral-700 text-white font-semibold px-6 py-3 hover:border-neutral-500 transition" %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>
